0. Verification note (read first)

This document is drafted from the inter-organizational paper and the ACC case as provided. It has not been aligned against the A0–A10 source files. Where a conformance clause depends on a definition in A0–A10, the dependency is flagged [ref: Axx]. All such flags must be resolved against source before this document is circulated as normative.

Items marked [BLOCKING] cannot be conformance criteria until an open problem is resolved. A document that asserts conformance to a BLOCKING clause is non-conforming until the block is lifted.

---

1. Scope

This document specifies the conformance requirements for the inter-organizational layer of AGENTIC ST-T1024, comprising six artifacts:

· A11 — Agent Certificate
· A12 — Intent Envelope
· A13 — Counterparty Admission Protocol
· A14 — Action Vocabulary Mapping
· A15 — Refusal as Wire-Level Protocol
· A16 — Cross-Organizational Trace Ledger

It does not specify:

· The core ST-T1024 architecture (determinism, ternary logic, FIS triage, FSM scheduling, ROM/SRAM split) — see the core standard.
· The agentic extension A0–A10 (extended FSM, ROM-burned action library, VERIFY gate, tile invocation protocol, persistent deterministic memory, goal parsing and plan library, bounded task execution, Verifiable Agency Theorem).
· The ACC concrete case.
· The trust authority. Conformance to this document does not imply or require any particular trust model. §8 of the inter-organizational paper specifies that the standard defines the format and the verification procedure; the market decides the authority.

---

2. Normative language

This document uses the RFC 2119 keywords:

· MUST — absolute requirement.
· MUST NOT — absolute prohibition.
· SHOULD — recommended; deviation requires justification recorded in the audit log.
· SHOULD NOT — not recommended; adoption requires justification recorded in the audit log.
· MAY — optional.
· CONFORMANCE BLOCKING — a clause that cannot yet be assessed because a named open problem is unresolved. An implementation that claims conformance to a BLOCKING clause is non-conforming until the block is lifted.

An implementation conforms to this document iff it satisfies every MUST and MUST NOT clause, and iff it does not claim conformance to any BLOCKING clause whose block is unresolved.

---

3. Conformance levels

Two levels are defined:

Level I — Intra-organizational conformance. The agent's behavior set B is finite and enumerable per the Verifiable Agency Theorem [ref: A0, A10], and the agent operates under the A0–A10 machinery. This level is a prerequisite for Level II. It does not require any artifact from A11–A16.

Level II — Inter-organizational conformance. The agent's operator, the counterparty, and the user each publish the artifacts specified in §4–§9 below. Level II requires Level I.

An implementation MUST declare its conformance level explicitly. An implementation that does not declare a level is non-conforming.

---

4. A11 — Agent Certificate

4.1 Required contents

A conforming Agent Certificate MUST contain:

· (a) B — the verified behavior closure of the agent, per Level I [ref: A10].
· (b) P — the plan library, with policy declared for each plan (HALT / ROLLBACK / atomic).
· (c) C — the composition rules, Δt bounds, and window W.
· (d) The hazard-to-predicate matrix — every declared hazard mapped to a predicate, a window, and a derived threshold.
· (e) The Environment Assumption Register (EAR), with all three layers declared (§4.4 below).
· (f) The mode declaration — sovereign or compositional — with the corresponding proof obligation met.
· (g) The refusal floor ε — the projected asymptotic refusal rate.
· (h) The ROM hash, the version, and the Friday signature chain.

4.2 Signature chain

The certificate MUST be signed. The signature chain MUST terminate at three named signers per Friday session (see §10). Each signer's role MUST be declared (Author / Verifier / Approver). The Author of a session MUST NOT be the Verifier of the same session. The Verifier of a session MUST NOT be the Approver of the same session. The Approver of a session MUST NOT be the Author of the same session.

4.3 Format

The certificate MUST be in a public, open format. A proprietary certificate format is non-conforming.

The format SHOULD use JSON Schema for structure, CBOR for canonical serialization, and Ed25519 for signatures. Alternative formats MAY be used if they are public and open, and if they support the same semantics.

4.4 EAR layers

The EAR MUST declare three layers:

· Layer 1 — Direct bounds. Per-source error bounds (ε_merchant, ε_payment, ε_inventory, etc.).
· Layer 2 — Cross-source consistency. Disagreement threshold δ_declared, with the refusal condition declared.
· Layer 3 — Predictive validity. Horizon and tolerance π, with the divergence condition declared.

Each layer MUST be a monitored assumption, MUST be signed by the Friday board, and MUST have a refusal condition specified.

4.5 Versioning

The certificate MUST be versioned. A certificate MUST be re-evaluated for admission on each Friday burn. A counterparty MAY re-evaluate more frequently.

4.6 Conformance claim

An implementation conforms to A11 iff every clause in §4.1–§4.5 is satisfied. A certificate that omits any of (a)–(h) is non-conforming. A certificate that omits ε is non-conforming.

