8. A15 — Refusal as Wire-Level Protocol

8.1 Required codes

A conforming implementation MUST support the following refusal codes as first-class protocol messages:

· 0x-AMBIG-INTENT — action outside the user's envelope.
· 0x-AMBIG-GOAL — no plan in P covers this goal.
· 0x-AMBIG-ACTION — action not in B, or not in the mapping.
· 0x-AMBIG-STATE — precondition failed on current state.
· 0x-AMBIG-TRACE — trajectory predicate violated.
· 0x-AMBIG-EPISTEMIC — EAR assumption violated.
· 0x-AMBIG-BUDGET — T-AET or spend cap exceeded.
· 0x-AMBIG-STAFFING — fewer than three qualified signers available (see §10.4).

8.2 Contractual meaning

Each refusal MUST be contractually meaningful. The refusing party MUST bear liability if the refusal is incorrect. An incorrect refusal MUST be treated as a breach.

8.3 Adjudication

The determination of whether a refusal is correct requires an adjudicator.

[BLOCKING — O2] The adjudicator is not specified in the inter-organizational paper. Conformance to A15 cannot be assessed until the adjudicator is named — arbiter, court, mutual third party, or other mechanism.

An implementation MUST NOT claim conformance to A15 while O2 is unresolved.

8.4 Non-gameability

The protocol MUST be non-gameable. A protocol in which the refusing party can escape liability by refusing without justification is non-conforming. A protocol in which the refused party can escape liability by inducing a refusal without cause is non-conforming.

8.5 Conformance claim

An implementation conforms to A15 iff every clause in §8.1–§8.4 is satisfied and O2 is resolved.

