11. Conformance claim format

An implementation claiming conformance MUST publish a conformance statement containing:

· The conformance level claimed (I or II).
· For Level II: the certificate identifier, the version, the ROM hash, and the signature chain.
· A list of the A11–A16 clauses claimed, with evidence for each.
· A declaration of the BLOCKING clauses not claimed (O1, O2) and the reason.
· The date of the statement and the version of this document against which conformance is claimed.

A conformance statement that does not declare BLOCKING clauses is non-conforming.

---

12. What conformance does NOT mean

Conformance to this document does not mean:

· That the agent is safe. It means the agent's behavior is bounded and the inter-organizational protocol is satisfied.
· That the trust model is sound. §8 of the inter-organizational paper leaves the trust authority to the market.
· That all hazards are covered. Coverage is with respect to the declared hazard inventory.
· That refusal is impossible. ε > 0 in open environments.
· That liability is resolved. Liability is traceable via the ledger; its legal enforcement is a matter for the applicable jurisdiction.
· That the platform will admit the agent. Admission is the platform's decision, made under its published protocol.

Conformance is a technical claim about artifacts and protocols. It is not a commercial, legal, or political claim.

---

13. Versioning

This document is versioned. A conformance statement MUST declare the version against which conformance is claimed. A change to a MUST clause invalidates prior conformance claims until re-assessed.

The Friday burn updates the certificate, not this document. This document is updated on a separate cadence, by the same three-role process specified in §10.

